# =============================================================================
# .trivyignore — Temporary Trivy suppressions
# =============================================================================
#
# Rules:
#   1. Every entry MUST have an expiration date (exp:YYYY-MM-DD).
#   2. Every entry MUST link to an upstream tracking issue/advisory.
#   3. When an entry expires, re-evaluate: remove it if the CVE is fixed,
#      or extend the date with a new justification if the fix still isn't
#      available. Never extend without re-checking upstream.
#   4. Keep the file short. A growing ignorefile is a smell.
#   5. Verify against the pinned CI Trivy (the `version` default in
#      .github/actions/install-trivy/action.yml) and its DB source —
#      local `brew install trivy` uses
#      the ghcr.io DB which can classify severities differently from the
#      CI's mirror.gcr.io/aquasec/trivy-db:2.
#
# Format:
#   CVE-XXXX-XXXXX exp:YYYY-MM-DD
# =============================================================================

# CVE-2026-17106 (GHSA-hfg8-hc9c-6c3h) — github.com/moby/go-archive v0.2.0: a
# crafted tar archive can write outside the extraction directory during
# untar. Statically linked into the docker-buildx CLI plugin that
# Dockerfile.dev installs (ARG BUILDX_VERSION). Surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on the GCO dev image — the only scanned image
# that carries the Buildx plugin. Fixed in moby/go-archive 0.3.0.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the GHSA was published
# 2026-08-18T21:17Z and reached the Trivy DB in the following hours. The
# Security workflow was green on main at eeeab67 (2026-08-18T20:50Z), 27
# minutes before publication, so this appeared on an unchanged buildx binary.
#
# Not clearable by a stable pin bump: re-checked 2026-11-01 against the now
# current stable Buildx v0.38.1 (2026-10-20), which this repository pins.
# Upstream published go-archive v0.3.0 on 2026-07-30 (v0.3.3 by 2026-08-05),
# but Buildx has not adopted it — v0.38.1 still links v0.2.0, verified with
# `go version -m` on the published linux-amd64 binary (checksum matched the
# release's checksums.txt), and the CI-pinned Trivy v0.76.1 still reports it
# there. The advisory range is `< 0.3.0`, so neither the v0.37.1 -> v0.38.0
# nor the v0.38.0 -> v0.38.1 bump clears or worsens the finding.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flagged path is tar extraction. buildx is a
# build-time CLI plugin that extracts contexts and cache artifacts authored by
# the contributor or CI invoking it, plus the version-pinned images it pulls —
# not attacker-supplied archives. The dev image is a contributor/CI tool that
# is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when a buildx release ships requiring moby/go-archive >= 0.3.0: bump
# BUILDX_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (the monthly deps-scan tracks it) and drop
# this entry. Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-17106
CVE-2026-17106 exp:2026-12-14

# Three undici advisories, all fixed upstream in undici 6.29.1, 7.30.1 and
# 8.12.2, and all present only as the undici 6.29.0 vendored inside npm's own
# node_modules in the dev image (for node-gyp's header downloads), surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev:
#   * CVE-2026-109869 (GHSA-xjj6-f33q-929c): undici does not handle an error
#     on a response body stream, so a server that breaks the stream mid-body
#     can crash the client process.
#   * CVE-2026-109383: undici's Agent dispatcher deep-clones its constructor
#     options through JSON before handing them to each per-origin pool, which
#     silently drops a caller-supplied tls.checkServerIdentity callback, so a
#     client that relies on it to validate certificates loses that check.
#   * CVE-2026-87018: the client process crashes when a TLS 1.2 server
#     renegotiates and presents a different, CA-trusted certificate whose
#     hostname does not match the request.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: CVE-2026-109869 was published
# 2026-10-23T22:14Z (the Security workflow was green on main at 42721a63,
# 2026-10-23T12:51Z, and first failed on PR #484, whose diff does not touch
# the dev image's npm); the other two were published 2026-10-15T19:17Z and
# first failed PR #494 after a green run on its base 2a318e7d
# (2026-10-26T20:14Z) — the ids reached the Trivy DB in between.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: re-checked 2026-11-01 by reading
# node_modules/undici/package.json inside the published npm tarball. npm
# 12.2.1 (2026-10-15, the newest release and the ARG NPM_VERSION this
# repository now pins) vendors undici 6.29.0, one patch below the fix. The
# same bump from 12.2.0 did re-vendor brace-expansion 5.0.12 and lift undici
# past 6.28.1, which retired the CVE-2026-102276, CVE-2026-102278 and
# CVE-2026-19534 entries this file used to carry.
#
# Low runtime risk: npm uses undici's fetch only for node-gyp downloading Node
# headers from the official dist server, with no custom checkServerIdentity to
# drop and no TLS 1.2 renegotiation from that server; the impact is
# availability of a contributor/CI command, and the dev image is never
# deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors undici >= 6.29.1: bump ARG
# NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev together with the packageManager pins and drop
# all three ids. The three expire together so they are re-evaluated in one
# pass.
# Advisories: https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xjj6-f33q-929c
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-109383
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-87018
CVE-2026-109869 exp:2026-12-27
CVE-2026-109383 exp:2026-12-27
CVE-2026-87018 exp:2026-12-27

# CVE-2026-114613: the msgpack-python C extension leaks a decoded map value
# when deserialization rejects a key under strict_map_key=True (the default),
# so repeated malformed input grows process memory without bound. Fixed in
# msgpack 1.2.3. The same vendored copy as GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 below —
# pip/_vendor/vendor.txt in python:3.14.9-slim pins msgpack==1.1.2 — in the
# dev image only: the Lambda images (lambda/helm-installer,
# lambda/tls-certificate-manager) now uninstall pip after installing their
# requirements, as the service images always have, so their scans no longer
# see pip's vendor manifest at all (confirmed 2026-10-28 with a local build
# and `trivy image` of each: Python target, zero findings).
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: published 2026-10-22T19:17Z; the
# Security workflow was green on main at 2a318e7d (2026-10-26T20:14Z) and
# first failed on PR #494, whose diff does not touch Dockerfile.dev.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump, re-checked 2026-11-01: pip 26.2.2, the newest
# pip on PyPI, still vendors msgpack 1.1.2. Both bases now ship that pip —
# python:3.14.9-slim (the FROM this repository pins) and
# public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 as rebuilt on 2026-10-31 — and their
# pip/_vendor/vendor.txt files were read directly to confirm it. pip's main
# branch has re-vendored 1.2.3, so the next pip release clears it, and until
# then no python:3.14.x-slim rebuild can carry a fixed pip. The dev image
# keeps pip because contributors install with it.
#
# Low runtime risk: the only reader is cachecontrol serialising pip's own
# HTTP cache while pip talks to a package index — a crash of pip at worst —
# and the dev image is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when pip re-vendors msgpack >= 1.2.3: pull a base image carrying that
# pip, confirm with `trivy image` on the base alone, then drop this id (and
# GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 below with it). Expires with the pip vendor.txt entries
# below so all of them are re-evaluated in one pass.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-114613
CVE-2026-114613 exp:2026-12-14

# CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools 70.3.0, fixed 78.1.1) and GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
# (msgpack 1.1.2, fixed 1.2.1) — reported by security:trivy:container-scan
# against the built dev image, under its python-pkg target.
#
# Provenance, established 2026-09-08 and correcting the AWS CLI attribution
# this entry previously carried: both come from pip's vendored dependency
# manifest in the base image, at
# usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/vendor.txt, which pins
#   msgpack==1.1.2
#   setuptools==70.3.0
# Trivy's python-pkg analyzer parses that file, which is why neither package has
# a dist-info of its own and neither appears in `pip list` — they are vendored,
# not installed. Reproduced on the completely unmodified base image:
#   trivy image python:3.14.7-slim
# reports exactly these two ids and nothing else, with no repository content,
# no AWS CLI and no apt package involved.
#
# Not clearable by any pin we control — re-checked 2026-11-01 on the base this
# repository now pins: python:3.14.9-slim ships pip 26.2.2, which is the
# newest pip on PyPI (2026-10-20), and its vendor.txt still pins both affected
# versions (public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14, rebuilt 2026-10-31, ships the
# same pip). `pip install --upgrade pip` is a no-op here, and no base-image
# bump can help because no fixed pip exists yet. pip's main branch has since
# re-vendored msgpack 1.2.3 (so the GHSA clears with the next pip release)
# but still pins setuptools 70.3.0. APT_SECURITY_EPOCH cannot reach this
# either: it only refreshes .deb packages, and this is a python-pkg finding.
#
# The earlier AWS CLI attribution was wrong, and bumping AWSCLI_VERSION
# 2.36.36 -> 2.36.41 on the strength of it did not clear either id. Recorded so
# the dead end is not re-walked: the 2.36.41 bundle and its installed
# /usr/local/aws-cli layout contain one dist-info (prompt_toolkit), zero
# egg-info, zero PKG-INFO, no msgpack file and no file containing "70.3.0", and
# a pinned-Trivy scan of that bundle is clean. Our own tree is clear too —
# msgpack appears nowhere in requirements-lock.txt and setuptools only as a
# commented "considered unsafe" marker.
#
# Low runtime risk, and for setuptools the vulnerable code is not even shipped:
#   * CVE-2025-47273 is a path traversal in setuptools.package_index.PackageIndex.
#     pip vendors pkg_resources, not setuptools — there is no
#     pip/_vendor/setuptools directory in the image, so the flagged module is
#     absent and vendor.txt records the version for provenance only.
#   * msgpack 1.1.2 code is present at pip/_vendor/msgpack, reached only through
#     cachecontrol serialising pip's own HTTP cache while pip talks to a package
#     index. Impact is a crash of pip (availability only, no code execution).
#   * The dev image is contributor/CI tooling that is never deployed to a
#     cluster, and neither path runs as a network service.
#
# Clears when pip re-vendors msgpack >= 1.2.1 and setuptools >= 78.1.1 (or drops
# the setuptools line): pull a base image carrying that pip, confirm with
# `trivy image` on the base alone, then drop both ids. Because the finding is
# entirely upstream of this repository, a published VEX statement would be the
# durable alternative to re-dating this entry.
#
# Advisories: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-47273
#             https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2026-12-14
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-12-14

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-97687 (traffic interception via an HTTPS proxy TLS configuration
# override) and CVE-2026-97689 (denial of service through unbounded memory
# allocation in the chunked-transfer parser) — urllib3 2.7.0, fixed in 2.8.0.
# Reported by security:trivy:container-scan against the dev image
# (Dockerfile.dev) and the two Lambda images built on
# public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 (helm-installer, tls-certificate-manager),
# under the pathless "Python (python-pkg)" target.
#
# Provenance, established 2026-10-01: the same mechanism as the entry above.
# pip 26.2.1 vendors urllib3 and records it in
# pip/_vendor/vendor.txt as `urllib3==2.7.0`; Trivy parses that manifest, so
# the finding has no dist-info of its own. Reproduced on the unmodified base
# images: `pip --version` reports 26.2.1 in both python:3.14.7-slim
# (/usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages) and public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14
# (/var/lang/lib/python3.14/site-packages), and both vendor.txt files pin
# urllib3==2.7.0. The images that keep no pip in their final stage do not
# report it. Our own urllib3 is 2.8.0 everywhere Trivy can see it:
# requirements-lock.txt, every lambda/*/requirements.txt, and each image's
# urllib3-2.8.0.dist-info scans clean in the same reports.
#
# Not clearable by any pin we control — re-checked 2026-11-01: pip 26.2.2
# (2026-10-20) is the newest pip on PyPI, both python:3.14.9-slim (the base
# this repository now pins) and public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 (rebuilt
# 2026-10-31) ship it with `urllib3==2.7.0` in their vendor.txt, and pip's
# main branch still vendors urllib3 2.7.0, so no fixed pip exists to upgrade
# to. A base-image bump cannot help until pip re-vendors, and
# APT_SECURITY_EPOCH only refreshes .deb packages.
#
# Low runtime risk: pip's vendored urllib3 runs only while pip itself talks to
# a package index, which the Lambda images never do after build and which the
# dev image does only while a contributor installs packages. Neither CVE is
# reachable through the application code, which imports the fixed 2.8.0.
#
# Clears when pip re-vendors urllib3 >= 2.8.0: pull a base image carrying that
# pip, confirm with `trivy image` on the base alone, then drop both ids. Expires
# together with the sibling pip vendor.txt entry above so the two are
# re-evaluated in one pass.
#
# Advisories: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-97687
#             https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-97689
CVE-2026-97687 exp:2026-12-14
CVE-2026-97689 exp:2026-12-14

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-78669 (GO-2026-6611): an HTTP/2 peer that opens many streams and
# then sends many small SETTINGS frames carrying SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE
# can drive a client or a server to excessive CPU use. Fixed in Go 1.26.9 and
# 1.27.2 (net/http) and in golang.org/x/net 0.60.0 (x/net/http2, 2026-10-08).
# Trivy reports the x/net copy under each Go binary's gobinary target in
# security:trivy:container-scan:
#   * Dockerfile.dev: kubectl v1.37.2 (x/net v0.57.0) and the docker-buildx
#     plugin v0.38.1 (x/net v0.59.0)
#   * lambda/helm-installer: kubectl v1.37.2 (as above)
# The stdlib half is fixed everywhere: the 2026-11-01 bumps moved kubectl to
# v1.37.2, Buildx to v0.38.1 and the Docker CLI to 29.9.2, all built with
# go1.26.10, the aws-lambda-rie in public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 (rebuilt
# 2026-10-31) is go1.26.9, and helm v4.3.1 is go1.27.2 with x/net v0.60.0.
# That retired the CVE-2026-78667 (net/http Range parsing) and CVE-2026-97031
# (crypto/tls ECH) entries this file carried for the same release.
#
# Provenance, re-established 2026-11-01: `go version -m` on the exact pinned
# linux-amd64 release artifacts, each checked against the sha256 trust anchor
# its Dockerfile carries (the Docker static tarball publishes none), reports
# the toolchains and x/net versions above, and the CI-pinned Trivy v0.76.1
# with the CI flags reports this id for kubectl v1.37.2 and buildx v0.38.1
# (x/net only) and for nothing else in the scanned set.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the Go advisory was published
# 2026-10-08T22:31Z and reached the Trivy DB hours after its two siblings;
# PR #451's Security runs scanned the same images clean of it as late as
# 2026-10-09T14:14Z (b6686e5c) with the kubectl and buildx pins of that day.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump, re-checked 2026-11-01: Kubernetes v1.37.2
# (2026-10-14) is the newest 1.37 patch and still links x/net v0.57.0; Buildx
# v0.38.1 (2026-10-20) is the newest Buildx release and links v0.59.0.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flaw reaches clients only from the HTTP/2 peer itself,
# which for these clients is a TLS endpoint they chose to call (the Kubernetes
# API or an image registry). The worst case is CPU exhaustion of one CLI
# process, or of one helm-installer invocation, which the Lambda timeout ends.
# The dev image is a contributor/CI tool that is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when kubectl and buildx link golang.org/x/net >= 0.60.0: bump
# KUBECTL_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev plus the kubectl URL and sha256 in
# lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile, and BUILDX_VERSION with both sha256
# anchors. Confirm each with `go version -m` on the binary (or `trivy image`),
# then drop the id.
# Advisories: https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6611
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-78669
CVE-2026-78669 exp:2026-12-14

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-56851 (GO-2026-6629): the Nickname profile in
# golang.org/x/text/secure/precis can panic with an out-of-bounds slice error
# when it transforms crafted input into a short destination buffer. Fixed in
# golang.org/x/text 0.41.0. security:trivy:container-scan reports it for
# kubectl v1.37.2, which links x/text v0.40.0, in Dockerfile.dev and
# lambda/helm-installer. No other scanned binary links an affected x/text.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the Go advisory was published
# 2026-10-07T14:09Z, but the Security run on main at 972fa63e (started
# 2026-10-16T18:34Z) still scanned both images clean. The first run to
# report it was PR #466's at 2026-10-19T10:17Z, with the same kubectl pin.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump, re-checked 2026-11-01: v1.37.2 (2026-10-14) is
# the newest Kubernetes 1.37 patch and, per `go version -m` on the
# checksum-matched release binary, still links x/text v0.40.0 (next patch
# target 2026-11-10).
#
# Not reachable: the vulnerable package is not compiled into kubectl. The
# release binary is stripped, but Go keeps every linked function's name for
# stack traces. Those names cover golang.org/x/text's cases, encoding,
# internal/language, language, runes, secure/bidirule, transform,
# unicode/bidi and unicode/norm packages, and nothing from
# golang.org/x/text/secure/precis, whose Profile and Transformer methods the
# advisory lists as the vulnerable symbols. govulncheck cannot confirm this
# on a stripped binary: it falls back to module-level matching and reports
# every symbol. Even where linked, the impact is a panic of one CLI process.
#
# Clears when a kubectl release links golang.org/x/text >= 0.41.0 (check with
# `go version -m`): bump it as the entry above describes, then drop this id.
# Expires with the entry above so the kubectl pins are re-evaluated in one
# pass.
# Advisory: https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6629
CVE-2026-56851 exp:2026-12-14

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-111943 (GO-2026-6809): Go 1.26.10 and 1.27.3 add a denylist to
# crypto/x509, so Certificate.Verify rejects a chain through any of eight
# revoked eMudhra intermediates. Trivy reports it as `stdlib` under each Go
# binary's gobinary target in security:trivy:container-scan:
#   * lambda/helm-installer: helm v4.3.1 (go1.27.2) and the base image's
#     aws-lambda-rie (go1.26.9)
#   * lambda/tls-certificate-manager: the base image's aws-lambda-rie
# The dev image no longer carries it: the 2026-11-01 bumps moved the Docker
# CLI to 29.9.2, kubectl to v1.37.2 and the docker-buildx plugin to v0.38.1,
# all built with go1.26.10 (`go version -m` on the checksum-matched release
# artifacts; the CI-pinned Trivy v0.76.1 reports none of them). The helm
# release artifact, matched to the sha256 anchor in
# lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile, carries go1.27.2; the aws-lambda-rie in
# public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 as rebuilt on 2026-10-31 carries go1.26.9.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the Go advisory was published
# 2026-10-21T00:27Z, and the Security run on main at 879bec75 (started
# 2026-10-20T21:56Z) still scanned all three images clean.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump, re-checked 2026-11-01: helm v4.3.1
# (2026-10-15) is still the newest Helm 4 release, and the newest upstream
# RIE, v1.37 (2026-08-31), predates the fix.
#
# Low runtime risk: the denylist matters only when a binary verifies a server
# chain against a trust store that still holds the root those intermediates
# chain to, and an on-path attacker holds one of the revoked keys.
#   * helm's Kubernetes calls verify the EKS API against the cluster CA in
#     the kubeconfig's certificate-authority-data, not the system store.
#   * helm's chart downloads in the helm-installer Lambda do use the system
#     store, and Amazon Linux 2023's ca-certificates
#     (2025.2.76-1.0.amzn2023.0.3, the newest in its repository on
#     2026-10-21) still carries emSign ECC Root CA - G3. That is the one
#     exposed path, from private subnets through the VPC's NAT gateways.
#   * aws-lambda-rie runs only when AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API is unset, which
#     means local emulation; deployed functions never start it.
#
# Clears when helm and the Lambda base's aws-lambda-rie are built with Go >=
# 1.26.10 (or >= 1.27.3): bump the helm URL and sha256 in
# lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile, confirm each binary's toolchain (or run
# `trivy image`), then drop the id.
# Advisories: https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6809
#             https://go.dev/issue/82345
CVE-2026-111943 exp:2026-12-21
